{
  "passage": {
    "id": "psg-water-cycle",
    "title": "The Water Cycle",
    "grade": 6,
    "text": "Water is always moving. When the sun heats a lake or an ocean, some of the water at the surface turns into water vapor and rises into the air. This process is called evaporation. As the vapor climbs higher, the air around it gets colder, and the vapor changes back into tiny droplets of liquid. This is condensation, and it is how clouds form.\n\nInside a cloud, droplets bump into each other and join together. When they become heavy enough, they fall as precipitation: rain, snow, sleet, or hail, depending on the temperature. Some of the water soaks into the ground, where plants can use it. The rest flows over the land into streams and rivers, and eventually the water returns to the lake or the ocean where it started.\n\nScientists call this the water cycle. It has no beginning and no end, and the same water has been moving through it for billions of years."
  },
